  "configuration_id": "config_042",
  "reason": "Evidence: pca_n2000 clearly outperforms scgpt (0.579/0.573 vs 0.494), and among the two pca_n2000+resnet50+transformer configs, concat fusion (config_006, 0.579) edges out weighted (config_003, 0.573). All three completed experiments used resnet50 as the image encoder, so the impact of a stronger image backbone remains entirely untested. Question: config_042 (pca_n2000, imagenet_vit_l, transformer, concat) is a clean single-variable swap from the best config (config_006), replacing resnet50 with the much larger ImageNet-pretrained ViT-Large while holding RNA encoder, aggregation, and fusion at their proven-best settings. This directly tests whether a richer image representation can push accuracy above the current ~0.58 ceiling. Rationale: With 6 experiments remaining, identifying whether the image encoder is a bottleneck is the highest-value uncertainty to resolve. If ViT-Large helps, it opens a productive exploitation path (e.g., testing avgpool or scvi_n200 with ViT-Large); if it does not, we can confidently focus on aggregation/fusion/profile-setting variations with resnet50. Expected relative cost: ViT-Large (~300M params) is substantially larger than ResNet50 (~25M params), so I estimate ~10–14 GPU hours versus the ~6.7 for config_006 — roughly 1.5–2× the cost of the current best. This is a justified investment given the potential accuracy gain and the 6 experiments still available."
}

